---
import InsightArticle from '../../layouts/InsightArticle.astro';
import ColumnChart from '../../components/insights/ColumnChart.astro';
import DataNote from '../../components/insights/DataNote.astro';
import insights from '../../data/insights/2026-11-14.json';

const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const base = rawBase.endsWith('/') ? rawBase : `${rawBase}/`;

const series = insights.outputInputRatio.series;
const first = series[0];
const last = series[series.length - 1];
const pct = (a: number, b: number) => `${Math.round((a / b) * 100)}%`;
const snapDate = new Date(insights.snapshotDate).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
const usd = (v: number) => `$${v.toFixed(2)}`;

// Ratio for each active, priced model in the snapshot.
const active = insights.priceSpread.all.map((m) => ({ ...m, ratio: m.output / m.input }));
const round2 = (v: number) => Number(v.toFixed(2));
const buckets = [
  { label: 'Under 2x', test: (r: number) => r < 2 },
  { label: '2x to under 4x', test: (r: number) => r >= 2 && r < 4 },
  { label: '4x to under 5x', test: (r: number) => r >= 4 && r < 5 },
  { label: 'Exactly 5x', test: (r: number) => r === 5 },
  { label: 'Over 5x', test: (r: number) => r > 5 },
].map((b) => ({ label: b.label, count: active.filter((m) => b.test(round2(m.ratio))).length }));
const fiveOrMore = active.filter((m) => round2(m.ratio) >= 5).length;
const lowest = [...active].sort((a, b) => a.ratio - b.ratio)[0];
const highest = [...active].sort((a, b) => b.ratio - a.ratio)[0];
const anthropic = active.filter((m) => m.provider === 'Anthropic');
const anthropicAllFive = anthropic.every((m) => round2(m.ratio) === 5);

const pick = (name: string) => active.find((m) => m.name === name)!;
const examples = ['Mistral Nemo', 'Llama 3.3 70B Instruct', 'Claude Haiku 4.5', 'Claude Fable 5', 'GPT-5.5', 'GPT-5.5 Pro', 'GPT-5.4 Nano', highest.name]
  .filter((n, i, a) => a.indexOf(n) === i)
  .map(pick)
  .filter(Boolean);

// Worked examples at GPT-5.5's list price.
const ref = pick('GPT-5.5');
const job = (label: string, tokensIn: number, tokensOut: number, note: string) => {
  const costIn = (tokensIn / 1_000_000) * ref.input;
  const costOut = (tokensOut / 1_000_000) * ref.output;
  return { label, tokensIn, tokensOut, costIn, costOut, total: costIn + costOut, share: costOut / (costIn + costOut), note };
};
const jobs = [
  job('Summarise a long report', 50_000, 1_000, 'Reads a lot, writes a little'),
  job('Draft a long article', 2_000, 8_000, 'Reads a little, writes a lot'),
  job('Short answer after long reasoning', 2_000, 21_000, '1,000 tokens of answer plus 20,000 of reasoning'),
];
const n = (v: number) => v.toLocaleString('en-GB');
---

<InsightArticle
  title="AI models now charge five times more to write than to read"
  description="The median new language model in our catalogue charges five times as much for output tokens as for input tokens, up from three times in late 2024. What the data shows and which kinds of work it makes dearer."
  date="2026-11-14"
  type="Analysis"
  readTime="7 min"
  snapshot={insights.snapshotDate} standfirst="Every model has two prices: one for the text it reads and one for the text it writes. Among models from late 2024 the writing price was typically three times the reading price. Among this year's models it is five."
>
  <p>
    Price lists for AI models quote two numbers per million tokens. The input price covers everything you send: your question,
    your documents, the conversation so far. The output price covers what the model writes back. Output has always cost more.
    The gap between the two has widened.
  </p>
  <p>
    We divided each model's output price by its input price and took the median for models released in each half-year. For
    models from the second half of 2024 the median is {first.median}. For models from the second half of 2025 onwards it is {last.median}.
  </p>

  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat"><b>{first.median}x</b><span>median output-to-input ratio, models from {first.period}</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{last.median}x</b><span>median ratio, models from {last.period}</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{pct(first.atLeast5x, first.n)}</b><span>of {first.period} models charged 5x or more</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{pct(last.atLeast5x, last.n)}</b><span>of {last.period} models charge 5x or more</span></div>
  </div>

  <ColumnChart
    title="Median output price as a multiple of input price, by release period"
    summary={`Median ratio by half-year: ${series.map((p) => `${p.period} ${p.median}x`).join(', ')}.`} maxValue={6} height={280} data={series.map((p, i) => ({
      label: p.period,
      value: p.median,
      valueLabel: `${p.median}x`,
      sub: `${p.n} models`,
      highlight: i === series.length - 1,
    }))} caption={`Source: AI Resource Hub catalogue, snapshot of ${snapDate}. Uses each model's current price, grouped by the half-year it was released. 2026 H2 runs to 14 November.`}
  />

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption>Source: AI Resource Hub catalogue, snapshot of {snapDate}. Models with both prices above zero.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Released</th><th class="num">Models</th><th class="num">Median ratio</th><th class="num">Charging 5x or more</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {series.map((p) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{p.period}</td>
            <td class="num">{p.n}</td>
            <td class="num">{p.median}x</td>
            <td class="num">{p.atLeast5x} ({pct(p.atLeast5x, p.n)})</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The median has sat at five since the second half of 2025, but the last column kept moving. In late 2024 about one model
    in six charged five times or more for output. Since July this year it is seven in ten.
  </p>

  <h2>Five has become the house number</h2>
  <p>
    Among the {active.length} models we list as active and priced, {fiveOrMore} charge five times or more for output, and {buckets[3].count} charge exactly five.
  </p>

  <ColumnChart
    title="Active language models by output-to-input price ratio"
    summary={`Models per ratio band: ${buckets.map((b) => `${b.label}: ${b.count}`).join('; ')}.`} height={280} data={buckets.map((b) => ({ label: b.label, value: b.count, valueLabel: String(b.count), highlight: b.label === 'Exactly 5x' }))} caption={`Source: AI Resource Hub catalogue, snapshot of ${snapDate}. Count of models out of ${active.length}.`}
  />

  <p>
    {anthropicAllFive && (
      <>
        Anthropic is the plainest case. All {anthropic.length} of its models in this set are priced at exactly five to one,
        from Claude Haiku 4.5 at $1 in and $5 out to Claude Fable 5 at $10 and $50.{' '}
      </>
    )}
    OpenAI's current models sit a little higher, at about six. The lowest ratio in the set is {lowest.name}, which charges
    the same for both, and the highest is {highest.name} at {highest.ratio.toFixed(1)} to one.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption>Source: AI Resource Hub catalogue, snapshot of {snapDate}. US dollars per million tokens.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Model</th><th class="num">Input</th><th class="num">Output</th><th class="num">Ratio</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {examples.map((m) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{m.name} <span style="color:var(--color-text-muted)">({m.provider})</span></td>
            <td class="num">${m.input}</td>
            <td class="num">${m.output}</td>
            <td class="num">{m.ratio.toFixed(1)}x</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The models near one to one are small open-weight models sold by third-party hosts. The models at five and six are the
    labs' own flagships. We can see that pattern in the prices. We cannot see the labs' costs, so we are not going to guess at
    why they price this way.
  </p>

  <h2>Which work this makes dearer</h2>
  <p>
    A higher output price only bites when a job writes a lot. Here are three jobs priced at {ref.name}'s list price of
    ${ref.input} per million input tokens and ${ref.output} per million output tokens. The token counts are made-up round
    numbers to show the arithmetic, not measurements.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption>
        Illustration only. Token counts are assumed. Prices are {ref.name} list prices from the snapshot of {snapDate}.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Job</th><th class="num">Tokens in</th><th class="num">Tokens out</th><th class="num">Input cost</th><th class="num">Output cost</th><th class="num">Output share</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {jobs.map((j) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{j.label}<br /><span style="color:var(--color-text-muted);font-weight:400;font-size:0.8rem">{j.note}</span></td>
            <td class="num">{n(j.tokensIn)}</td>
            <td class="num">{n(j.tokensOut)}</td>
            <td class="num">{usd(j.costIn)}</td>
            <td class="num">{usd(j.costOut)}</td>
            <td class="num">{pct(j.costOut, j.total)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The summary costs {usd(jobs[0].total)} and nine tenths of that is input. The output ratio barely matters. The article
    costs about the same, {usd(jobs[1].total)}, and almost all of it is output.
  </p>
  <p>
    The third row is the one to watch. OpenAI's <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/reasoning" rel="noopener">documentation on reasoning models</a> (read on 14 November 2026) says the tokens a model spends thinking are not shown to you but "are billed as output tokens",
    and that a response can use "anywhere from a few hundred to tens of thousands" of them. So a short answer from a model
    that reasons first can cost more than a long article from one that does not. In our made-up example it comes to {usd(jobs[2].total)}, and you only ever see 1,000 of the 21,000 tokens you paid for.
  </p>
  <p>
    So if you use a reasoning mode, the output price is the one to check. The thinking is charged at the output rate, and
    the output rate has moved from three times the input rate to five.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you pick on price</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Look at the output price first</strong> if your work is writing, coding or anything that uses a reasoning mode.
      Look at the input price first if you mostly feed in long documents and ask short questions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check what a "blended" price assumes.</strong> Sites that quote a single price per million tokens, ours
      included, have to assume a mix of input and output. Ours assumes one part input to three parts output and the <a href={`${base}transparency/#calc-blended-price`}>formula is published</a>. If your mix is different, the blended
      figure will mislead you.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Measure a real job.</strong> Run your own task once, read your provider's usage report for that request, and
      only then scale it up. The <a href={`${base}calculator/`}>cost calculator</a> will
      do the multiplication.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <DataNote anchor="calc-output-input-ratio" snapshot={`${snapDate} (catalogue rebuilt ${insights.cacheGeneratedAt.slice(11, 16)} UTC)`}>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>What we counted.</strong> For the trend, every language model with a release date and both prices above zero,
        duplicates removed, grouped by half-year of release. Groups with fewer than 10 models are left out, which removes
        everything before July 2024. For the second chart, the {active.length} models we mark as active.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Current prices, not launch prices.</strong> The trend uses what each model costs today. If an older model's
        prices have changed since launch, its ratio here reflects the new prices.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Odd rows.</strong> Five models in the trend data show ratios between 12 and 971 to one. They are priced from
        third-party hosts and the largest look like data errors, so we have flagged them to be checked. A median is not moved by a few
        extreme values, and none of them is in the active set.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Release dates.</strong> For most models the date is the day OpenRouter first listed it.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </DataNote>
</InsightArticle>
